\begin_layout Section
Using your own Hamiltonian
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The nearest-neighbour chain of the Model section is only one choice. Nothing else in SANCHO depends on it: the Green's functions, bubbles, self-energies, SCF loop and observables see nothing but the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

. Any single-particle Hamiltonian can therefore be used, as long as it follows the rules below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
What the matrix must look like
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Storage.
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 is the module variable 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
H
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
src/hamiltonian.f90
\end_layout

\end_inset

, declared 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
complex(dp), allocatable :: H(:,:)
\end_layout

\end_inset

. The main program allocates it as 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
H(Nsites, Nsites)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and passes it to the routine that builds it, as in 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
call build_chain_hamiltonian(H, Nsites, E_CC, t_hop)
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Basis.
\series default
 One orbital per site, index 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 = site 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $i = 1,\dots,$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Nsites
\end_layout

\end_inset

. The size comes from the input key 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Nsites
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Spin-independent.
\series default
 SANCHO assumes the same 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 for both spins (a paramagnetic chain; the current carries a factor 2). Spin-dependent terms (spin–orbit coupling, magnetic fields) cannot be expressed here; use QUICHOTTE for those.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Hermitian.
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $H = H^\dagger$
\end_inset

, with a real diagonal. SANCHO has no 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
make_hermitian
\end_layout

\end_inset

, so fill both triangles, e.g.
\begin_inset space \space{}

\end_inset

by setting 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
H(j,i) = conjg(H(i,j))
\end_layout

\end_inset

 after each off-diagonal element. 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
info_Hamiltonian.dat
\end_layout

\end_inset

 lists 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{ij}-H_{ji}^*$
\end_inset

 for every pair; all must be zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Units and energy zero.
\series default
 Energies in eV, on the same scale as 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
mu
\end_layout

\end_inset

: at 
\begin_inset Formula $V=0$
\end_inset

 the levels below 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 are filled.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Static and non-interacting.
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 must not depend on energy or on the SCF iteration. The interaction enters only through the Hubbard 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 of each site, so do 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 put Hubbard or Hartree terms into 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

: they would be counted twice.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Contacts.
\series default
 The left lead couples to site 1 and the right lead to site 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Nsites
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (strengths 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GammaL
\end_layout

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GammaR
\end_layout

\end_inset

). Number your sites so that the contacted ones come first and last, or change the two lines that set 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GammaL
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GammaR
\end_layout

\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
src/sancho.f90
\end_layout

\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Anything else is allowed. Hoppings may connect any pair of sites and have any range, on-site energies may differ from site to site, and the matrix may be dense. The bubbles and the second- and third-order self-energies are computed for every pair of sites, so they do not assume any particular connectivity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Step by step
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Write the routine inside 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
module Hamiltonian
\end_layout

\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
src/hamiltonian.f90
\end_layout

\end_inset

. A template (a chain with alternating hoppings 
\begin_inset Formula $t_1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t_2$
\end_inset

; replace the body with your model): 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
   subroutine build_my_hamiltonian(HCC, n, ECC, t1, t2)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
      !...my model: chain with alternating hoppings t1, t2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
      integer, intent(in) :: n
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
      real(dp), intent(in) :: ECC, t1, t2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
      complex(dp), intent(out) :: HCC(n, n)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
      integer :: i
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim

\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
      HCC = (0.d0, 0.d0)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
      do i = 1, n
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
         HCC(i, i) = ECC                       !...on-site energy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
      end do
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
      do i = 1, n - 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
         if (mod(i, 2) == 1) then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
            HCC(i, i + 1) = t1                 !...bond i -> i+1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
         else
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
            HCC(i, i + 1) = t2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
         end if
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
         HCC(i + 1, i) = conjg(HCC(i, i + 1))  !...keep H Hermitian
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
      end do
\end_layout

\begin_layout Verbatim
   end subroutine build_my_hamiltonian
\end_layout

